<span id="S5.T4.5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-4o</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T4.5.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:284.5pt;">
<span id="S5.T4.5.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">0:[mailin, ballot, absentee], 1:[medicare, social security, trump], 2:[musk, media, elon], 3:[latino, hispanic, conservative], 4:[midterm elections, midterm, midterms]</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the election dataset, all LLMs could match reasonably well in election discourse in general. When we look at specifics, we find none of the LLMs could match popular topic clusters related to Ukraine and Russia. Continuing in this trend, we observe that while real content has women and abortion as popular topics, other LLMS picks up other social groups like <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latino-Hispanic</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gay</span> by GPT-4o and Claude-3.5 models, respectively. Given that we focus exclusively on popular topics, these themes are present across all datasets but in less popular proportions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Popular topic clusters (top-3 important words per cluster) in real and synthetic influencers dataset. Synthetic datasets struggle to capture all popular topics in real data.</span></figcaption>
